Tested in scratch repos: a missing -F file, a blank -m, a staged
change plus an untracked file, two commits with the same subject,
-F - from stdin, and the autosquash with stock git 2.56.0 and the
local build. When the rebase runs with --autostash, the staged change
comes back unstaged; that is how rebase applies its autostash, not
something this script does.

The scratch message files live under the git dir
(rev-parse --git-path reword-msg, reword-full) rather than mktemp, to
match interactive-staging, which keeps its state there too.
